From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: server: an owner set on a users-writable machine key keeps the users' access

A fresh OfficeSetup.exe install, signed in, could not save a new Word
document ("The save failed due to out of memory or disk space"), the failure
5942 fixed. The keys of Click-to-Run's machine hive (HKLM\Software\Microsoft\
Office\ClickToRun\REGISTRY) had the machine's descriptor again: SYSTEM and
Administrators full, users read. Office's virtual registry inside WINWORD.EXE
opens them with KEY_ALL_ACCESS as the user and was refused.

Keys of that hive (and of the display configuration) that have no descriptor
are writable by users, and a key created there gets the users-writable DACL.
But Click-to-Run's service, as SYSTEM, sets an owner on its hive's keys (the
owner only). Setting part of a descriptor on a key that had none fills the
rest from the token: the token's default DACL, which for SYSTEM and, since
7805, the admin token too, is the machine's. Such a key now takes the
users-writable DACL, as when it is created there. Other keys, and a DACL the
caller gives, are unchanged.

Gate: test-hklmsd (SYSTEM sets the owner of two keys of the hive loaded
without a descriptor; a standard user still opens them with KEY_ALL_ACCESS).
Mutant SG_MUTANT_C2R_SETSD_TOKEN_DACL.

